  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>Two Grant firms made waggons and farm implements in <ref target="civic?id=Berkley%20Street">Berkley Street</ref>, and a Grant &amp; Co. is listed there until 1920. G. Grant, waggon builder, is listed from 1865 to 1868, and Geo. Grant &amp; Co., waggon builders and agricultural implement makers, from 1869 to 1871, then as G. Grant &amp; Co. from 1872 to 1880. James Grant &amp; Co., agricultural implement makers, are listed beside them from 1869 to 1871, and again in 1885 and 1890. In 1895 J. Grant &amp; Co., implement makers, are also at No. 570 <ref target="civic?id=Elizabeth%20Street%20North">Elizabeth Street North</ref>.</p>
        <p>From 1895 to 1920 the directories list Grant &amp; Co., agricultural implement makers, at No. 31 Berkley Street from 1900. They don't say which of the two firms this was.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>J. F. Hyne, waggon builder, is next to G. Grant in 1865, and George Notman, coach trimmer, is next to G. Grant &amp; Co. from 1872 to 1880. Later neighbours are Prosser Bros., livery stables, at No. 13 (1900), the <ref target="civic?id=Nicholson%20implement%20makers">Nicholson &amp;amp; Morrow</ref> store yard (1910), Stamp &amp; Powell, engineers, at Nos 41–47 (1915), and W. Bertram &amp; Son, blacksmiths and engineers, at Nos 25–27 (1920).</p>
      </div>
